[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, i.e. unoptimized, gcc/glibc on x86-64 Linux).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Program under test: `translation/src/main.rs`, built with `cargo build --release`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Comparison method: both programs are spawned as subprocesses with the same bytes on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdin; stdout, stderr and exit status are compared byte for byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`translation/tests/differential.rs` + `translation/tests/harness/mod.rs`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust code is never loaded as a library.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No behavioral mismatch was found.** Every input class enumerated below produces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m identical stdout, identical stderr (always empty) and the same exit status (always 0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in both programs. The sections below record what was checked, the C semantics that had
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to be reproduced for that to be true, and how the tests were proven to be capable of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m detecting a mismatch.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void bad()  { int *data;  printIntPtrLine(data); }        /* uninitialized pointer */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void good() { int data = 5; int *p = &data; printIntPtrLine(p); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int  main() { int x = 0; scanf("%d", &x); if (x) good(); else bad(); return 0; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` has one branch, but `scanf("%d", &x)` supplies many distinct input classes that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m select it. `good()` prints `5\n`; `bad()` is the undefined-behavior path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Hazards that had to be reproduced, and how each was confirmed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `bad()` dereferences an uninitialized `int *` (CWE-457)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is undefined behavior, so there is no "correct" value to print — only what the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reference build actually does. Disassembly of the reference binary shows `bad()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m loading its pointer straight out of an untouched stack slot:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bad:  sub $0x10,%rsp ; mov -0x8(%rbp),%rax ; mov %rax,%rdi ; call printIntPtrLine
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The slot holds residue from the preceding `scanf` call frame. Empirically the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints `0\n` and exits 0, **deterministically**. Confirmed stable across:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - 25 repeated runs of the same input,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - environment-block padding of 0 / 1 / 100 / 4000 bytes (shifts the initial stack),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - absolute vs. relative `argv[0]`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - ~40 differently shaped `bad()`-reaching inputs (EOF, matching failure, parsed zero,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   truncated-to-zero, 64 KiB of junk), which drive materially different glibc `scanf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   code paths, including ones that grow an internal work buffer.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The translation therefore models the read as `0`, behind a named constant
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`UNINITIALIZED_POINTER_READ`) so the origin of the value is not mistaken for program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m logic. Note this is a *pinned observation of undefined behavior*, not a guarantee: on a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m different compiler, optimization level or libc the C program could print something else
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m or fault. The tests compare against the C program on every run, so such a divergence
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m would surface as a test failure rather than being silently accepted.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `scanf("%d")` skips whitespace, including newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%d` skips leading `isspace()` bytes before converting, so it reads *across* newlines —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unlike `fgets`. All six C-locale space bytes must be skipped:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `' '`, `'\t'`, `'\n'`, `'\v'` (0x0b), `'\f'` (0x0c), `'\r'`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified with `"   \n\n  7"` → `5\n` (`good()`) and with 65 536 newlines followed by `7`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A mutation that narrowed the whitespace set to `' '`/`'\t'` was detected by the suite.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. A failed conversion leaves `x` at its initializer, so `x == 0` → `bad()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m EOF (empty input, `/dev/null`, closed fd), whitespace-only input, a non-numeric first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte, and a sign not followed by a digit (`"+"`, `"-"`, `"+ 5"`, `"--5"`) all leave `x`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m untouched at `0`. The C program does **not** check `scanf`'s return value, so all of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m these silently take the `bad()` path — no error message, exit 0. Reproduced by returning
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `None` from `scanf_int` and leaving `x` at `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. glibc converts `%d` through `strtol`: saturation, then truncation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the subtlest part, and it changes which branch runs:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Values outside `long` range saturate at `LONG_MAX` / `LONG_MIN` (they do **not** wrap).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - The saturated `long` is then **truncated** into the `int` argument.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Consequences that were confirmed against the C binary:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin | `long` value | stored `int` | branch | stdout |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` | 2147483648 | -2147483648 | `good()` | `5\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` (2^32) | 4294967296 | **0** | `bad()` | `0\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `8589934592` (2^33) | 8589934592 | **0** | `bad()` | `0\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372032559808512` | as written | **0** | `bad()` | `0\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775807` (`LONG_MAX`) | `LONG_MAX` | -1 | `good()` | `5\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `18446744073709551616` (2^64) | saturates to `LONG_MAX` | -1 | `good()` | `5\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775808` (`LONG_MIN`) | `LONG_MIN` | **0** | `bad()` | `0\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-18446744073709551616` | saturates to `LONG_MIN` | **0** | `bad()` | `0\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
